<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Base numbers only. The per day growth in Days.xml multiplies hp and contactDamage, except where
  ignoreScaling is set: the boss figures are absolute so "9999 x 3" reads exactly as written.

  Every enemy is slower than the player's 4.5. Pressure comes from being surrounded, never from being
  outrun, and Deadline at 4.0 sits right against that ceiling so it always feels like a near miss.

  reportVerb / reportUnit feed the end of run work log, which is what turns a kill tally into
  "处理邮件 328 封".

  knockbackCd is the internal cooldown on the knockback debuff, defaulting to 1.2s. It is what stops
  six weapon slots from chaining pushes into permanent displacement, and it is the only limiter that
  does not care how many slots are firing, which is why it and not the per weapon knockback distance
  is the knob to reach for. At 0.6s a keyboard build pushed a body 1.3 units a second against its own
  2.2 of walking speed, so nothing ever arrived. The two heavy bodies take longer ones: a boss shoved
  around by a stapler is just a large mail.
-->
<Enemies>

  <Enemy id="mail" name="邮件" reportVerb="处理邮件" reportUnit="封"
         hp="12" speed="2.2" contactDamage="6" exp="2" radius="0.34"
         viewId="v_mail" tier="Normal" />

  <Enemy id="deadline" name="Deadline" reportVerb="赶上 Deadline" reportUnit="次"
         hp="8" speed="4.0" contactDamage="12" exp="2" radius="0.30"
         viewId="v_deadline" tier="Normal"
         behavior="SuicideOnContact" />

  <!--
    Killing one makes two, so clearing faster puts more on screen instead of less. It is the only
    enemy whose pressure scales with the player's own damage.

    The brood is thrown backwards, away from the player, across splitArc degrees. splitSpread was
    0.55 with the children placed opposite each other, which meant one of the two always landed
    between the corpse and the player: inside the badge orbit, inside the keyboard blast, and dead
    before it was drawn. The joke of this enemy is watching one become two, and it does not survive
    the second one never being seen.

    splitInvuln buys that look. It is short enough to defend nothing and long enough to be read.
  -->
  <Enemy id="bug" name="BUG" reportVerb="修复 BUG" reportUnit="个"
         hp="10" speed="2.8" contactDamage="5" exp="2" radius="0.33"
         viewId="v_bug" tier="Normal"
         behavior="SplitOnDeath"
         behaviorParam="splitInto=bug_small;splitCount=2;splitSpread=1.15;splitArc=110;splitInvuln=0.2" />

  <!-- Zero exp: 修 BUG 不算 KPI. It is also what stops splitting from wrecking the exp curve. -->
  <Enemy id="bug_small" name="小 BUG" reportVerb="修复 BUG" reportUnit="个"
         hp="4" speed="3.4" contactDamage="3" exp="0" radius="0.20"
         viewId="v_bug_small" tier="Normal" />

  <Enemy id="ppt" name="PPT" reportVerb="看完 PPT" reportUnit="份"
         hp="45" speed="1.2" contactDamage="10" exp="4" radius="0.42"
         viewId="v_ppt" tier="Normal"
         behavior="AuraMoveSlow" behaviorParam="radius=3.2;pct=25" />

  <!--
    The only ranged unit. Without it, running in circles wins every day and movement stops meaning
    anything. Projectile speed 5 against the player's 4.5 keeps every shot dodgeable.
  -->
  <Enemy id="report" name="周报" reportVerb="提交周报" reportUnit="份"
         hp="18" speed="1.4" contactDamage="6" exp="3" radius="0.36"
         viewId="v_report" tier="Normal"
         behavior="RangedKeepDistance"
         behaviorParam="interval=2.5;damage=8;projSpeed=5;range=10;keepDistance=6.5" />

  <!-- Never attacks. It does not threaten your life, only your throughput. -->
  <Enemy id="veteran" name="老油条" reportVerb="说服老油条" reportUnit="人"
         hp="80" speed="1.0" contactDamage="0" exp="5" radius="0.46"
         viewId="v_veteran" tier="Normal"
         behavior="AuraAttackSlow" behaviorParam="radius=3.6;pct=25" />

  <!--
    speed 3.0 rather than 1.8. At 1.8 the elite was slower than the mail it buffs and slower than a
    BUG, so the one enemy with 300 hp and an aura was also the one you could walk away from and
    forget, which is the opposite of what an elite is for. 3.0 clears mail at 2.2 and BUG at 2.8, so
    it closes while its haste pushes the swarm ahead of it, and still leaves 1.5 of headroom under
    the player's 4.5: kiting it stays a decision rather than becoming a reflex.
  -->
  <Enemy id="leader" name="领导" reportVerb="应付领导" reportUnit="次"
         hp="300" speed="3.0" contactDamage="16" exp="15" radius="0.62"
         viewId="v_leader" tier="Elite" knockbackCd="2"
         behavior="AuraHaste" behaviorParam="radius=4.5;pct=30" />

  <!--
    Three bars of 9999. Phase two only shortens cooldowns and widens the folder throw, phase three
    adds the rain, so the whole escalation is two branches rather than three separate fights.

    contactDamage 52 rather than 20, and the reason is that ignoreScaling freezes damage as well as
    health. The flag exists so "9999 x 3" reads exactly as written, but it also opted the boss out of
    the per day growth every other enemy gets, and by Saturday dmgScale is 2.6: a Deadline hit for
    31 and 领导 for 42 while the boss, which only ever appears on that one day, still hit for 20.
    52 is 20 x 2.6, so the figure written here is the figure that lands. Change dmgPerDay in Days.xml
    and this one has to be recomputed by hand, because opting out of the formula is what the flag is.

    phaseAddId and summonId are weighted rosters, "id:weight,id:weight". A bare id still works and
    weighs one. Both used to be a single id, which meant the two loudest moments of the fight always
    produced the same silhouette: twenty identical mails on a bar break, four identical PPTs on every
    meeting.

    The rosters are picked for what they do to the auto lock, not for flavour. Every weapon targets
    the nearest body, so the bar break wave is all cheap fast shapes that die on contact with the
    six slots and clear out again: putting sponges in a twenty six body wave would park the whole
    loadout on the wave and leave the boss untouched for the rest of the bar. 老油条 and PPT appear
    only in the meeting, where the count is small and being briefly walled in is the point of the
    skill. 周报 rounds it out because a meeting where everyone reports is the joke, and because it is
    the one summon that keeps threatening from outside the ring the player is trying to leave.

    画饼 lands its circles on the boss rather than on the player, which is the opposite of what 甩 KPI
    does and deliberately so. KPI has to follow the player or it cannot hit anybody; the pies are a
    tax on the ground next to the boss, and the two together are what stops the fight being readable
    as "stand anywhere and hold still". The slow is applied at cast and the circles resolve pieWarn
    later, so the skill bills the player for the distance the slow just took away from them.

    pieDamage is a share of contactDamage so it tracks the one figure on this row that was hand
    computed against dmgScale. 0.5 sits well under the 0.9 the KPI circles take because those land
    on the player's head with no way to be out of position, while a pie is only ever collected by
    somebody who chose to be next to the boss.
  -->
  <Enemy id="boss" name="老板" reportVerb="挺过述职" reportUnit="次"
         hp="9999" speed="1.4" contactDamage="52" exp="60" radius="0.95"
         viewId="v_boss" tier="Boss" ignoreScaling="true" knockbackCd="4"
         behavior="BossSkills"
         behaviorParam="bars=3;phaseInvuln=2;phaseAdds=26;phaseAddId=mail:5,deadline:3,bug:3,report:2;pieCd=15;pieSlowPct=30;pieSlowPctLate=40;pieDuration=3;pieCount=6;pieCountLate=8;pieWarn=1;pieBlast=1.6;pieInner=1.8;pieOuter=5.2;pieDamage=0.5;meetingCd=16;summonId=ppt:2,report:2,veteran:1,deadline:1;summonCount=6;summonRadius=3;summonWarn=1;kpiCd=8;kpiCount=3;kpiCountLate=5;kpiBlast=1.8;kpiWarn=0.8;kpiScatter=2.2;rainInterval=1.5;rainCount=4;rainWarn=0.7;rainBlast=1.5" />

</Enemies>
